<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rafael Carrera</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1814">1814</a>–<a href="/years/1865">1865</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rafael Carrera was a Guatemalan caudillo of humble origins who in 1837 led a peasant revolt against the liberal government of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federal-republic-of-central-america">Federal Republic of Central America</a>. His victory helped break up the federation, and he dominated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a> from 1839, intervening in El <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salvador">Salvador</a> and Honduras. President from 1844 to 1848 and from 1851, he was made president for life in 1854 and ruled until his death in 1865 as a conservative ally of the Church.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rafael-carrera.jpg" alt="Rafael Carrera" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unidentified painter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Carrerayturcios_2014-06-22_09-46_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From 1837 <b>Rafael Carrera</b>, a young mestizo swineherd, led an insurgency of peasants and indigenous villagers in eastern Guatemala, using guerrilla tactics against government troops.</blockquote><a href="/empire/federal-republic-of-central-america">Federal Republic of Central America · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>When <b>Rafael Carrera</b>’s conservatives defeated Francisco Morazán at Guatemala City in March 1840, the federation collapsed, and on 18 February 1841 a constituent assembly adopted a constitution declaring El Salvador a sovereign and independent state.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-el-salvador">Republic of El Salvador · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Liberal reforms under the state governor Mariano Gálvez provoked a peasant revolt in 1837, inflamed by a cholera epidemic and by attacks on the church, which brought the young mestizo leader <b>Rafael Carrera</b> to prominence.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-guatemala">Republic of Guatemala · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Morazán’s last attempt to restore the federation ended with his execution in Costa Rica in 1842, and conservative generals allied with <b>Rafael Carrera</b> of Guatemala, notably Francisco Ferrera, the first constitutional president, dominated the following years.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-honduras">Republic of Honduras · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After Morazán's defeat by <b>Rafael Carrera</b> in 1840, El Salvador lost its federal role, and a constitution of February 1841 declared it a sovereign state, the Republic of El Salvador.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-el-salvador">State of El Salvador</a></li><li><blockquote>When cholera struck in 1837, rural Maya and ladino communities rose under <b>Rafael Carrera</b>, who took Guatemala City in 1838.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-guatemala">State of Guatemala</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala-city">Guatemala City</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/guatemala">Guatemala</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala-city">Guatemala City</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/guatemala">Guatemala</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=14.64,-90.51&amp;year=1847" aria-label="Guatemala City on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2075 1619 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Rafael Carrera"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2525 1889h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2525 1889h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=14.64,-90.51&amp;year=1847">What was here in 1847? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
